msgid ""
msgstr ""
"# Layer Normalization Example\n"
"\n"
"This document provides a detailed example of layer normalization. Layer normalization is a technique used to stabilize and accelerate the training of deep neural networks. Unlike batch normalization, layer normalization computes the mean and variance across all features of a single sample, making it independent of batch size and particularly suitable for recurrent neural networks and transformer models.\n"
"\n"
"## Mathematical Definition\n"
"\n"
"Given an input vector \\( x \\in \\mathbb{R}^d \\), layer normalization computes:\n"
"\n"
"\\[\n"
"\\hat{x} = \\frac{x - \\mu}{\\sqrt{\\sigma^2 + \\epsilon}} \\odot \\gamma + \\beta\n"
"\\]\n"
"\n"
"Where:\n"
"- \\( \\mu = \\frac{1}{d} \\sum_{i=1}^{d} x_i \\) is the mean of the input features.\n"
"- \\( \\sigma^2 = \\frac{1}{d} \\sum_{i=1}^{d} (x_i - \\mu)^2 \\) is the variance of the input features.\n"
"- \\( \\epsilon \\) is a small constant added for numerical stability (e.g., \\( 1e-5 \\)).\n"
"- \\( \\gamma \\) and \\( \\beta \\) are learnable parameters (scale and shift) of the same dimension as \\( x \\).\n"
"\n"
"## Implementation Example\n"
"\n"
"Below is a simple implementation of layer normalization using PyTorch:\n"
"\n"
"```python\n"
"import torch\n"
"import torch.nn as nn\n"
"\n"
"class LayerNorm(nn.Module):\n"
"    def __init__(self, d_model, eps=1e-5):\n"
"        super(LayerNorm, self).__init__()\n"
"        self.gamma = nn.Parameter(torch.ones(d_model))\n"
"        self.beta = nn.Parameter(torch.zeros(d_model))\n"
"        self.eps = eps\n"
"\n"
"    def forward(self, x):\n"
"        mean = x.mean(-1, keepdim=True)\n"
"        var = x.var(-1, keepdim=True, unbiased=False)\n"
"        x_norm = (x - mean) / torch.sqrt(var + self.eps)\n"
"        return self.gamma * x_norm + self.beta\n"
"```\n"
"\n"
"## Usage Example\n"
"\n"
"```python\n"
"# Create a layer normalization module\n"
"layer_norm = LayerNorm(d_model=512)\n"
"\n"
"# Input tensor of shape (batch_size, sequence_length, d_model)\n"
"x = torch.randn(2, 10, 512)\n"
"\n"
"# Apply layer normalization\n"
"output = layer_norm(x)\n"
"print(output.shape)  # Output shape: torch.Size([2, 10, 512])\n"
"```\n"
"\n"
"## Comparison with Batch Normalization\n"
"\n"
"| Feature | Layer Normalization | Batch Normalization |\n"
"|---------|-------------------|-------------------|\n"
"| Normalization dimension | Across features of a single sample | Across the batch dimension for each feature |\n"
"| Dependency on batch size | Independent | Dependent |\n"
"| Suitable for RNNs | Yes | No |\n"
"| Suitable for Transformers | Yes | Yes (with modifications) |\n"
"| Computation cost | Lower | Higher |\n"
"\n"
"## Advantages and Disadvantages\n"
"\n"
"### Advantages\n"
"- Works well with small batch sizes.\n"
"- Suitable for sequence models and transformer architectures.\n"
"- No need to maintain running statistics during inference.\n"
"\n"
"### Disadvantages\n"
"- May not perform as well as batch normalization in some convolutional neural network tasks.\n"
"- Requires careful initialization of the learnable parameters.\n"
"\n"
"## Conclusion\n"
"\n"
"Layer normalization is a powerful and widely used technique in modern deep learning. It is particularly effective for models that process sequential data or have varying batch sizes. By normalizing the activations across the feature dimension, it helps stabilize training and improve convergence speed.\n"

